Initialize the EditContext before the first input.

A new editor at the default line 1, column 1 sent no cursor-change event,
so its EditContext stayed empty when the keyboard connected. On the base
Android build with Gboard 12.4 and WebView 134, accepting a file from Quick
Open left that empty buffer focused until the first key. The letter's
compositionstart then filled it and the editor restarted input. Typing one
a before SEED BETA 1 produced aSEEDSEEDSEEDSEED BETA 1: the keyboard
recomposed the word against the first letter's range instead of the word's
current range.

At the end of NativeEditContext construction, read the view model's primary
selection, start the buffer at its first line, and synchronize the native
text, selection and caches through the existing update method. An explicit
initialization argument excludes this write from queuing an input restart,
even if the host is focused before the next microtask. Later updates use
the default argument and keep the existing input, selection and restart
rules. The first compositionstart finds the text already synchronized.

Every editor using NativeEditContext gets this initialization on every
supported platform, including diff and peek editors, Source Control and
chat inputs where enabled, and read-only and unfocused editors. It copies
the primary selection's lines: one at the default caret, more for an
existing multiline selection. A distant caret does not copy the preceding
file, but a very long line still costs its full length. Restoring a saved
view later can replace this first window and can queue an ordinary input
restart; only the initial call is excluded. The write changes neither the
document nor focus and sends no screen-reader notification. An invalid
model selection retains the existing no-update behavior. Textarea inputs
and the plain Quick Open input are unchanged. The eager copy's main-thread
cost and assistive behavior have not been measured.

With both editor changes built, on API 33 with Gboard 12.4 and WebView 134,
three Quick Open runs focused the new buffer with SEED BETA 1 and selection
0..0 already present; one a left aSEED BETA 1 each time. No updateText filled
the buffer during that letter's compositionstart, and no blur from
workbench.js occurred during its input sequence. These runs used the
constructor initialization, without a helper priming the new context or
refocusing it. On API 36 with Gboard 15.9 and WebView 149, where the keyboard
did not underline the word, the same open-and-type sequence left aHello
World! from Hello World!. With 0048 also built, on an API 34 emulator with
Gboard 12.4 and WebView 157, three runs left aSEED BETA 1; a build without
this change had put the letter at offset 3 on an API 33 emulator with Gboard
12.4 and WebView 157 (SEEaD BETA 1, two runs).

This removes the empty-buffer and late-initialization steps. It does not
reject a recomposition with a stale native range if the keyboard still sends
one, nor does it correct a selection the keyboard explicitly requests.
Other NativeEditContext users are reached by the same constructor in the
source; the file-opening runs do not establish their device behavior.

